Section 4: MPEP Section 2163 — Written Description and Enablement (35 U.S.C. 112)

Every botanical patent application must provide full, clear, concise, and exact description of:
1. The precise botanical species and plant part utilized (e.g., Curcuma longa rhizome);
2. Standardized extraction solvent, temperature, yield, and HPLC/LC-MS chemical fingerprint;
3. Enablement allowing a person skilled in the art of pharmacognosy to reproduce the therapeutic effect without undue experimentation.

Section 1: EPC Article 52 — Patentable Inventions

(1) European patents shall be granted for any inventions, in all fields of technology, provided that they are new, involve an inventive step and are susceptible of industrial application.
(2) The following in particular shall not be regarded as inventions:
(a) discoveries, scientific theories and mathematical methods;
(b) aesthetic creations;
(c) schemes, rules and methods for performing mental acts, playing games or doing business;
(d) presentations of information.

### Traditional Medicine & Natural Substance Doctrine at the EPO
Under EPO Guidelines for Examination G-II, 3.1:
If a substance found in nature has first to be isolated from its surroundings and a process for obtaining it is developed, that process is patentable. Moreover, if this substance can be properly characterized by its structure, chemical fingerprint, or extraction parameters, and it has a technical effect, the substance per se may be patentable. However, mere natural extracts without technical modification are considered non-patentable discoveries.

Section 3: WIPO Diplomatic Treaty on IP, Genetic Resources and Associated TK (May 2024)

Adopted by consensus at the WIPO Diplomatic Conference in Geneva (May 24, 2024):
Article 3 — Mandatory Disclosure Requirement:
1. Patent applicants are required to disclose the country of origin of the genetic resources if the claims in a patent application are materially based on genetic resources;
2. Where the claims are materially based on traditional knowledge associated with genetic resources, applicants must disclose the indigenous peoples or local community who provided the traditional knowledge;
3. Information sharing systems: Contracting parties are mandated to establish digital information systems (databases of genetic resources and traditional knowledge, modeled after India's TKDL) in consultation with local communities, accessible to patent examiners worldwide to prevent wrongful patent grants.
